      "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
      "body": "I'm in favour of dropping (i.e. empty string) the user agent completely. It's a fingerprinting vector (incl. possible implications for more targeted attacks, although I think this is limited - an attacker can still fingerprint based on observed behaviour in many cases), and I am not aware of any actionable takeaways we should draw from this dataset. While it's fun to see (presumed) upgrade velocity, I don't think this actually influences Bitcoin Core's release policy. It seems a poor fit for influencing vulnerability disclosure too. We have no way of measuring how many of those nodes are abandoned, unused, or economically irrelevant. Furthermore, I think in the current security environment, we can no longer afford to wait for - or optimize our disclose practices around - laggards.\n\nThe main areas where having data is useful are for consensus upgrades and for network policy changes. For the former, we have miner signalling data. For the latter, we should measure observed behaviour (e.g. messages, feature flags, ...) instead.",
